<DocLayout title="API reference" eyebrow="Developer first" description="The small public surface for creating a matcher, applying updates, inspecting state, and asserting the invariant." next={{ label: "Run examples", href: "/examples/" }}>
  <h2>Constructor</h2>
  <pre><code>{`Matcher(n: int, mode: "basic" | "multilevel")`}</code></pre>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Argument</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>Number of vertices. Vertices are integers in <code>[0, n)</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mode</code></td><td>Exactly <code>basic</code> or <code>multilevel</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>insert(u, v)</code></td><td>Adds an existing or new graph edge and repairs the matching.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>delete(u, v)</code></td><td>Removes an existing graph edge and repairs any affected matching state.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Update semantics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>insert(u, v)</code> ignores a duplicate edge after validating its endpoints.</li>
    <li><code>delete(u, v)</code> records a missing-edge deletion without changing the graph.</li>
    <li>Self-loops are outside the graph model and do not become matching edges.</li>
    <li>A failed update is atomic: callers do not receive a partially applied state.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Inspection</h2>
  <pre><code>{`matcher.matching()  # matching edge snapshot
matcher.size()      # number of matching edges
matcher.maximal()   # invariant check
matcher.stats()     # update and rebuild ledger`}</code></pre>
  <p>Inspection methods are read-only from the caller’s perspective. Treat returned collections as snapshots and continue to mutate the graph through <code>insert</code> and <code>delete</code>.</p>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>Use</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>partner(v)</code></td><td><code>int | None</code></td><td>Return the matched neighbor or <code>None</code>.</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>partners()</code></td><td><code>dict[int, int]</code></td><td>Return a copy of the full partner map.</td><td>O(|M*|)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>matching()</code></td><td><code>set[Edge]</code></td><td>Return a copy of the current matching.</td><td>O(|M*|)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td><code>int</code></td><td>Return the number of matching edges.</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>maximal()</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td><td>Check the matching against the current graph.</td><td>O(n + m)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>stats()</code></td><td><code>dict[str, int]</code></td><td>Return counters and active schedule values.</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Validation and failure</h2>
  <p>Invalid vertices, malformed edges, and unsupported modes are rejected explicitly. Duplicate insertions and missing deletions follow the documented no-op semantics. A failed update must not expose a partially applied graph or matching state.</p>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Invalid <code>n</code>, vertex, mode, graph, or colorer</td><td><code>ValueError</code> before state changes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duplicate insertion, missing deletion, or self-loop update</td><td>Validated no-op; the graph and matching remain unchanged.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Internal repair or invariant failure</td><td><code>RuntimeError</code> after atomic rollback; inspect the diagnostic and preserve the input trace.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="doc-callout"><p>For reproducible experiments, record the mode, vertex count, update sequence, and <code>stats()</code> ledger alongside your result.</p></div>

  <h2>Custom implementations and concurrency</h2>
  <p>The optional <code>graph</code> and basic-mode <code>colorer</code> parameters are extension points for compatible implementations. A custom graph must preserve the graph protocol and edge-set semantics; a multilevel matcher always uses its canonical paper-oriented colorer.</p>
  <p><strong>Thread safety:</strong> a matcher is stateful and is not designed for concurrent mutation. Serialize updates per instance. Use independent matcher instances for parallel experiments.</p>
</DocLayout>
